# Mediator

Encapsulate how a set of objects interact. Colleagues know the mediator, not each other.

## Anatomy

- `notify(sender, event, data)`.
- Concrete mediator sequences work and rollback.
- Colleagues call `this.mediator.notify(...)` after their step.

```ts
export interface Mediator {
  notify(sender: string, event: string, data?: unknown): unknown;
}

export abstract class Colleague {
  protected mediator!: Mediator;

  setMediator(mediator: Mediator) {
    this.mediator = mediator;
  }
}
```

Wire after DI: inject colleagues into the mediator, then `colleague.setMediator(this)`.

Mediator may implement compensation (release inventory if payment fails).

## Nest mapping

| Facade (`workflows/`) | Mediator |
| --- | --- |
| Workflow calls A then B then C | A/B/C call back into the mediator |
| Subsystems stay ignorant of the flow | Colleagues are flow-aware via events |

Prefer **Facade workflows** in this repo (`CreateShareWorkflow`, `CompleteSpaceFileUploadWorkflow`). They already sequence use-cases without a notify protocol.

Use Mediator when several services must **broadcast to each other** without a web of imports. If they only need “something happened”, prefer Observer (`SpaceEventsService` / pubsub) so publishers stay unaware of subscribers.

Do not store per-request logs on a singleton mediator field (`currentLog`) without request scope — that races.

## When to use

- Dense many-to-many collaboration + shared rollback.
- Adding a colleague should not patch every other colleague.

## When not to use

- Linear orchestration — a workflow is enough.
- Mediator becomes a god object — split or use events.

Related: `nest-facade.mdc`, `nest-observer.mdc`, `nest-command.mdc`.
